Our parliamentary team publishes the reallocation rule and the PLN 50 ticket
with costs attached, then asks the activists to leave the lanes while the bill
receives a hearing. It is a proposal: opposition cannot fund it, and a Lewica
minister cannot bind the rest of a coalition by press release.

The activists gain a route other than asphalt but not the enactment they
demanded. PSL and KO object to the road budget and the unfunded national fare;
the SLD organisation objects to negotiating under blockade. The party spends
money and coalition access to ensure the policy cannot be buried beneath the
tactic.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the transport bill on the events desk

@bridges_observers
title: Fund legal observers, defend peaceful protest and endorse neither the blockade nor its demands
subtitle: Cost: 1 resource. Preserves a general civil-liberties rule while both internal camps resent the refusal to choose.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: A trained legal-observer rota needs one resource.
on-arrival: resources -= 1; last_generation_stance = "Legal observation without endorsement"; constitutional_restraint += 3; police_trust += 1; public_trust += 1; party_unity -= 2; progressives_dissent += 2; barons_dissent += 2; climate_pressure_unmet += 2; media_capacity -= 1; news_headline = "Lewica monitors the bridge removals and endorses neither side"

Party lawyers record arrests, force and threats from drivers, while our
statement says peaceful protesters retain rights even when the party rejects
their tactic. We do not adopt the motorway reallocation or the national fare.

The record may matter in court. Politically it leaves both questions open.
Activists see lawyers without solidarity; the SLD organisation sees party
money spent protecting people who stopped traffic. The middle course costs a
resource, unity and climate credibility rather than serving as an exit.

- @poland_event_queue: File the observer reports

@bridges_penalties
title: Call for rapid road clearance and escalating penalties for repeat blockades
subtitle: Wins order and party relationships; loses young organisers and deepens the unanswered climate conflict.
on-arrival: last_generation_stance = "Escalating penalties"; climate_activist_support -= 2; public_trust += 2; barons_dissent -= 3; progressives_dissent += 7; razem_dissent += 3; young_support -= 4; climate_pressure_unmet += 5; far_right_agenda += 2; ko_relation += 2; pis_relation += 2; psl_relation += 2; news_headline = "Lewica calls for escalating penalties against repeat road blockades"

We ask for fast, documented removal from live traffic and escalating fines for
repeat obstruction. The line is a political demand, not an operational order
to police, and it includes the same evidence and proportionality rules for
every protest movement.

Commuters hear an answer and the larger parties welcome it. Ostatnie Pokolenie
hears that disruption worked only well enough to make punishment bipartisan.
Progressive and younger supporters move sharply against the leadership while
the transport demands remain exactly where they were before the first person
sat down.

- @poland_event_queue: Back the penalty demand

@last_generation_wislostrada_2024
# prose-audit: Grade A — The repeated Wisłostrada blockades and promised crackdown force a final choice among costly mediation, solidarity and punitive alignment, with the previous stance visible in the room.
